From: ms178
Subject: [PATCH v2] Unified downstream patch set for glibc 2.44 (commit 7cba7779)

Rebased and semantically audited against glibc commit
7cba77790f3279bec3ac20e9c7632b021cd53f95 (2.44).
Tuned for Intel Raptor Lake (i7-14700KF) gaming workloads.

=====================================================================
v2 PERFORMANCE WORK  (all numbers from callgrind on this exact tree)
=====================================================================

Methodology note.  Wall-clock on the measurement box has a 74% spread
(same binary, same config, 15 runs: min 99.0 ms / p50 135.2 / max 172.7).
Callgrind instruction counts reproduce to 0.00002% (146,064,066 /
146,064,086 / 146,064,100).  Every performance claim below is therefore
stated in instructions retired (Ir), measured on a purpose-built
gaming-shaped workload (ECS-style churn, asset-key string ops, buffer
streaming, cross-thread producer/consumer free, frame teardown).

--- 1. TCACHE_FILL_COUNT 16 -> 256  (the main win) ------------------

  phase      count=16 (Ir)   count=256 (Ir)   delta
  teardown    146,064,044     116,440,466     -20.3%
  xthread     120,334,800     103,897,089     -13.7%
  churn        40,060,081      38,131,714      -4.8%

  Controlled: forcing glibc.malloc.tcache_count=16 on the patched build
  reproduces 146,067,953 Ir, i.e. the change is the sole cause (0.003%).

  Cost: peak RSS unchanged within noise (3084 -> 3144 KB, ~2%).
  Bound: worst-case small-bin retention is
  sum(tidx2csize(0..63)) * count = 34,304 * 256 = 8.4 MiB per thread,
  and only for a thread touching every size class.  num_slots[] is
  uint16_t and do_set_tcache_count() enforces MAX_TCACHE_COUNT ==
  UINT16_MAX, so 256 is far inside the safe range.

  Deliberately NOT raising tcache_max: measured at count=256, moving
  tcache_max 1040 -> 4096 REGRESSED teardown 116.4M -> 134.7M Ir
  (+15.7%) because large chunks displace the hot small-size classes.

--- 2. Removed the tcache_get_n() software prefetch -----------------

  Disassembly shows the prefetch retires 4 instructions before the
  function's 'ret', so the line cannot arrive earlier than the demand
  load issued by the *next* malloc call.  A/B over 50 interleaved runs
  (min and median) showed no effect outside noise; on teardown the
  prefetch build was actually the slower minimum (27.22 vs 26.83 ms).
  Removed rather than shipped on faith.

--- 3. Kept the _int_free_chunk() prefetches ------------------------

  These are differently placed: the prefetch at the top of the
  !SINGLE_THREAD_P path is followed by 'lock cmpxchg' before the merge
  touches the neighbour headers, so it genuinely overlaps the atomic's
  latency.  Cachegrind cannot score this (it models prefetch as a
  no-op) and the box has no PMU, so this is retained on the strength of
  the instruction-schedule argument, not a measured win.  Flagged as
  such rather than claimed as a speedup.

--- 4. Rejected: branchless largebin_index_64 -----------------------

  Implemented and exhaustively proven equivalent (0 mismatches over
  1024..64M, including SIZE_MAX).  Rejected on measurement: upstream's
  branch chain compiles to 6 instructions on the common path and the
  first branch is heavily biased, so the predictor makes it free.

    random sizes      : branchless 2.422 vs branchy 2.643 ns  (-8%)
    realistic band0   : branchless 2.434 vs branchy 2.251 ns  (+8%)
    realistic 90% b0  : branchless 2.432 vs branchy 2.151 ns  (+13%)

  Real large-chunk distributions are overwhelmingly band 0, so the
  "optimisation" is a pessimisation.  Upstream left as-is.

--- 5. Verified, no change needed: string IFUNC dispatch ------------

  The strings phase runs __strlen_avx2 / __strcmp_avx2 /
  __memcmp_avx2_movbe.  Checked whether EVEX variants should be
  selected: they require AVX512VL+AVX512BW, which Raptor Lake does not
  expose (AVX-512 is fused off).  AVX2 is therefore already optimal on
  the target CPU.  (On the AVX-512-capable measurement box glibc still
  picks AVX2, because Prefer_No_AVX512 is set when AVX-VNNI is absent,
  to avoid AVX-512 downclocking -- the dispatch logic is correct.)

=====================================================================
CORRECTNESS AUDIT FIXES (carried over from v1)
=====================================================================

 * malloc: large_csize2tidx() is deliberately left UNCLAMPED.  The old
   malloc.patch clamped it to TCACHE_MAX_BINS-1; because
   do_set_tcache_max() uses the out-of-range return value as its
   rejection test, that clamp made glibc.malloc.tcache_max ACCEPT
   oversized values (>=4 MiB) that upstream rejects, setting
   tcache_max_bytes far past the last bin so many distinct size classes
   alias onto entries[TCACHE_MAX_BINS-1].  Shown by differential test:
   tcache_max=4194304 goes from accept=0 (upstream) to accept=1
   (clamped).  The two formulas were also proven to agree across the
   entire legitimate range (1041..4194303), so the clamp bought nothing.
 * malloc: bounds now enforced at the five allocation call sites via the
   new tcache_idx_valid() helper -- including the __libc_free() large
   path, which previously read tcache->num_slots[tc_idx] before any
   upper-bound check (out-of-bounds read).
 * malloc: prefetch/free hunks re-expressed against the 2.44
   _int_free_chunk() structure (upstream removed the have_lock/errno
   save-restore logic the old patch anchored on).
 * malloc: tcache_get_n() keeps upstream's branch structure; 'mangled'
   is constant-folded at every call site via __always_inline, so the old
   "fold the ternary" rewrite bought nothing and obscured the mangling
   invariant.
 * inet/Makefile, support/Makefile: -fno-strict-aliasing blocks
   re-anchored (upstream moved the surrounding rules).

Rebase status of the previous series against 2.44: 7 of 11 patches no
longer applied (ms178-2, ms178-4, malloc, pthreadspinlocks, clear,
07-mandriva-nostrictaliasing, zstd-locales).  All consolidated here.

