+   tcache bin will hold at most this number of chunks.
+
+   ms178: raised from the upstream 16 to 256.  A larger per-thread cache
+   keeps far more freed chunks out of the arena, which avoids the
+   lock + unsorted-bin + consolidation path on the alloc/free storms that
+   dominate game frame teardown.  Measured with callgrind (deterministic
+   instruction counts; wall clock on the test box has ~74% noise) on a
+   gaming-shaped allocator workload:
+
+     phase      count=16 (Ir)   count=256 (Ir)   delta
+     teardown    146,064,044     116,440,466     -20.3%
+     xthread     120,334,800     103,897,089     -13.7%
+     churn        40,060,081      38,131,714      -4.8%
+
+   Peak RSS is unchanged within noise (3084 KB -> 3144 KB on the churn
+   phase, ~2%).  The worst-case small-bin retention is bounded at
+   sum(tidx2csize(0..63)) * count = 34,304 * 256 = 8.4 MiB per thread,
+   and only for a thread that actually touches every size class.
+   num_slots[] is uint16_t and do_set_tcache_count() enforces
+   MAX_TCACHE_COUNT == UINT16_MAX, so 256 is well inside the safe range.
+
+   NOTE: deliberately NOT raising tcache_max.  Measured: with count=256,
+   raising tcache_max 1040 -> 4096 moved teardown from 116.4M to 134.7M Ir
+   (+15.7%), because large chunks displace the hot small-size classes.  */
+# define TCACHE_FILL_COUNT 256
 
 /* Maximum chunks in tcache bins for tunables.  This value must fit the range
    of tcache->num_slots[] entries, else they may overflow.  */
@@ -1728,6 +1751,8 @@ free_perturb (char *p, size_t n)
 
